So what's an "official" word?

All languages share a very large international vocabulary, words
like peritonitus, lepidoptera or calcium carbonate.  The number
of such words is enormous, to the point that it makes any
difference with regards to other words statistically irrelevant,
if they are counted.  And I don't think you'd get over a million
words in any language without counting them.  If you're counting
such words, and counting the presence of words in published
works, then English probably would rank first.  Not because it
has a larger vocabulary, but simply because there are far more
technical publications in English than in any other language.
Most of the worlds languages probably don't have a single
published work with one of the words above, but the words exist
just as much in the language as they do in English; they've just
not found their way into published literature.

